<section class="page-intro section-wrap">
  <p class="eyebrow">The playing guide / 01 &mdash; 04</p>
  <h1>So, how do<br><em>you play it?</em></h1>
  <p class="lede">You choose a chord, then move your pen across the tablet to strum it. Here's how that works, how to change chords, and what you'll need to get sound out of it.</p>
  <a class="text-link" href="/about/getting-started/">Need to get connected first? Start here <span aria-hidden="true">&nearr;</span></a>
</section>

<section class="story-row section-wrap" aria-labelledby="strum-title">
  <figure>{{ illustration("strum", "A stylus strums across the virtual strings on a drawing tablet") }}<figcaption>01 / Move across the strings to play notes</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="story-copy">
    <p class="eyebrow">01 / Strumming</p>
    <h2 id="strum-title">Strum with<br><em>your pen.</em></h2>
    <p>The tablet acts like a set of virtual strings. You can <a href="/playing/strum/">choose how many to use</a>, then drag your pen across them. Move slowly to pick out notes, or sweep across them for a full chord.</p>
    <p><a href="/playing/strum/#make-it-expressive">Pressure, tilt, and position</a> can change the volume, pitch bend, and length of the notes. You can adjust what each one controls.</p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/playing/strum/">More about strumming <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="story-row story-reverse section-wrap" aria-labelledby="chords-title">
  <figure>{{ illustration("chords", "Three notes chosen on a keyboard form a chord") }}<figcaption>02 / Choose chords with buttons or keys</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="story-copy">
    <p class="eyebrow">02 / Chords</p>
    <h2 id="chords-title">Set your<br><em>chord.</em></h2>
    <p>Use a computer keyboard or <a href="/playing/chords/#a-chord-at-the-touch-of-a-button">tablet buttons to change chords</a>. Assign a chord to each button, or set up a progression to play through.</p>
    <p>You can also <a href="/playing/chords/#two-hands-one-instrument">play a chord on a MIDI keyboard</a> with one hand and strum it with the other.</p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/playing/chords/">Ways to change chords <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="story-row section-wrap" aria-labelledby="midi-title">
  <figure>{{ illustration("midi", "MIDI connects the tablet to a synthesizer") }}<figcaption>03 / Send the notes to a synth or DAW</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="story-copy">
    <p class="eyebrow">03 / MIDI</p>
    <h2 id="midi-title">Connect<br><em>your synth.</em></h2>
    <p>Run on a laptop, desktop, or Raspberry Pi. <a href="/playing/midi/">Connect to your favorite synth or DAW</a>, and pick a nice pluckable sound.</p>
    <p>Sketchatone doesn't make sound on its own. It sends MIDI notes, so you'll need an instrument track in your DAW or a hardware synth to hear anything.</p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/playing/midi/">MIDI setup basics <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="story-row story-reverse section-wrap" aria-labelledby="assemble-title">
  <figure>{{ illustration("assemble", "A tablet, compact computer, and strap form a DIY instrument") }}<figcaption>04 / A tablet and Raspberry Pi on a frame</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="story-copy">
    <p class="eyebrow">04 / Your setup</p>
    <h2 id="assemble-title">Put it<br><em>together.</em></h2>
    <p>A tablet on your desk is enough to get started. Put it beside a MIDI keyboard if you'd like to use the keys for chord changes.</p>
    <p>For a more DIY setup, you can <a href="/playing/assemble/">mount the tablet and a Raspberry Pi on a frame</a>, add a strap, and wear it like a guitar.</p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/playing/assemble/">What you'll need &amp; setup ideas <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
  </div>
</section>

